    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A coalition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, liberal and radical intellectuals and the bazaar forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> shah to accept a parliament in 1906, though the three groups were united by common enemies rather than common goals.</li><li>Clerics were on both sides: Akhund Khurasani and two other leading marja' in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a> backed the constitution, while Fazlollah Nouri opposed it and was hanged in July 1909.</li><li>Constitutionalists restored the constitution in 1909 after the Minor Tyranny, but the revolution ended in December 1911 when the Second Majlis was expelled with the support of 12,000 Russian troops.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The tobacco protest of 1891 to 1892 worked so well that Iranians stopped smoking despite their love of tobacco, and it was said that even the women of the shah's harem gave it up.</p></aside>
    </section>

