    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1891</span><span class="rf-text">The tobacco protest begins against a 50-year monopoly granted to an Englishman, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-hasan-shirazi">Mirza Hasan Shirazi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a> of December 1891 closes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> and stops the smoking of tobacco</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1905</span><span class="rf-text">Merchants and clergy protest against tariffs imposed to repay the Russian loan for the shah's tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1906</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar">Mozaffar ad-Din Shah</a> agrees to a parliament, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-consultative-assembly">National Consultative Assembly</a> first meets in October</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1907</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-russian-convention">Anglo-Russian Convention</a> divides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> into a Russian zone in the north and a British zone in the south</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1908</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah</a> stages a coup and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombard">bombards</a> the parliament, beginning the Minor Tyranny</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1909</span><span class="rf-text">Constitutionalist forces march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, force the shah to abdicate and re-establish the constitution; Fazlollah Nouri is hanged in July</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1910</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sattar-khan">Sattar Khan</a> refuses to disarm and is wounded in a confrontation with Yeprem Khan, the police chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1911</span><span class="rf-text">In December the shah's cabinet, backed by 12,000 Russian troops, expels the deputies of the Second Majlis</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-julius-reuter"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-julius-reuter.jpg" alt="Paul Julius Reuter" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-julius-reuter">Paul Julius Reuter</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reuter,_Paul_Julius_von,_Nadar,_Gallica.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nadar, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the late 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, like most of the Muslim world, suffered from foreign intrusion and exploitation, military weakness, lack of cohesion and corruption. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a> of 1813 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> of 1828 it lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and its Caspian navy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and paid an indemnity of three million pounds to the tsar; in the Treaty of Paris of 1857 it agreed to withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> shahs had no standing army, relying on tribal contingents, and sold titles, patents, concessions, monopolies and lands, spending part of the proceeds on the court. Mass-manufactured foreign textiles undermined the traditional handicrafts: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> not even a fifth of the weavers' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> survived the competition, and by 1894 the price farmers were paid for wheat had fallen to a sixth of its 1871 level.</p><p>A concession of 1872 gave a British citizen control over roads, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a>, mills, factories and mining in exchange for a fixed sum and 60% of net revenue; it was rolled back after bitter local opposition. In March 1890 Naser al-Din Shah granted Baron <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-julius-reuter">Paul Julius Reuter</a> a 50-year tobacco monopoly. Protest spread first among the bazaari and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, and in December 1891 the marja'-e taqlid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-hasan-shirazi">Mirza Hasan Shirazi</a> declared the use of tobacco tantamount to war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Hidden Imam</a>. Nikki Keddie traced a direct line from the coalition of the tobacco protest to the Constitutional Revolution. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904–05 and the Russian revolution of 1905 gave further impetus, as constitutions came to be seen as the secret of Western strength.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitutionalist movement rested on an alliance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, liberal and radical intellectuals and the bazaar, an alliance based on common enemies rather than common goals. The ulama sought to extend their own power and to have Shi'i <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> more strictly enforced; the liberals and radicals wanted greater political and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-democracy">social democracy</a> and economic development; and the bazaaris wanted to restrict the favoured economic status of foreigners and their competition. The intellectuals were a small but growing group, many of whom had learned of Western ways while travelling abroad and were struck by Western economic development, comparative justice and lack of arbitrary rule. In their writings they criticised autocratic rulers, petty officials, venal clerics, arbitrary courts and the low status of women.</p><p>The bazaari became convinced that law and order, security of property and immunity from arbitrary power could all be achieved by importing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The ulama had less to gain, but were convinced for a time that their position beside the monarchy would not be weakened. The revolutionaries argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s oil industry was being sold to the British, that tax breaks on imports, exports and manufactured textiles were destroying an economy sustained by the bazaar merchants, and that the shah was selling assets to pay interest on his foreign debt. The concrete demand became a parliament, a majlis, that would limit the power of the shah.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar.jpg" alt="Mozaffar ad-Din Shah Qajar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar">Mozaffar ad-Din Shah Qajar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mozaffar_ad-Din_Shah_Qajar_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Golestan Palace, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1905 protests arose over tariffs imposed to repay the Russian loan that had financed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar">Mozaffar ad-Din Shah</a>'s tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In December two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> merchants were bastinadoed for price-gouging, the merchants closed the bazaar and the clergy followed. The government dispersed protesters who had taken sanctuary in a mosque, and a larger movement took refuge at a shrine outside Tehran. On 12 January 1906 the shah agreed to dismiss his prime minister and transfer power to a house of justice. In a later fight government forces killed a sayyid, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> killed 22 protesters and injured 100; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> went on strike and many merchants took shelter at the British embassy. In the summer of 1906 about 12,000 men camped in its gardens, and the shah agreed to a parliament in August. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-consultative-assembly">National Consultative Assembly</a>, with 156 members elected mostly from Tehran and the merchant class, met in October; the constitution was modelled in part on that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah</a>, who opposed the constitution, came to power in January 1907, and in August the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-russian-convention">Anglo-Russian Convention</a> divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> into zones. In 1908 the shah staged a coup and bombarded the parliament, a time called the Minor Tyranny. Rebels held out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sattar-khan">Sattar Khan</a> and by April 1909 had driven out the royalist forces. Constitutionalists marched on Tehran, forced the shah to abdicate in favour of his young son Ahmad Shah and restored the constitution in 1909.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sattar-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sattar-khan.jpg" alt="Sattar Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sattar-khan">Sattar Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SattarKhanPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The clergy were divided. Three of the highest-ranking marja' of the time, Akhund Khurasani, Mirza Husayn Tehrani and Shaykh Abdullah Mazandarani, telegraphed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a> in favour of the constitution from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a>. Khurasani and his student Muhammad Hossain Naini argued that complete justice was impossible until the return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Hidden Imam</a>, but that democracy reduces the tyranny of the state and was a lesser evil that Shi'i Muslims must support. Sayyed Mohammad Tabatabai and Sayyed Abdullah Behbahani were the two most widely recognised clerical leaders in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> itself. Mohammed Kazem Yazdi stayed neutral most of the time, though he reviewed the draft constitution, suggested changes and signed it.</p><p>Fazlollah Nouri led the opponents. He held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a> was a complete code of life and that any other code was unnecessary and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and he told Shi'i Muslims to ignore the marja' they followed if that marja' supported democracy. Supporters named him a court official with a vested interest in the old order who had ties to the Russians. Nouri began a sit-in at the Shah Abdol-Azim Shrine on 21 June 1907 that lasted until 16 September 1907. The commanders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> resistance were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sattar-khan">Sattar Khan</a> and his lieutenant Bagher Khan; Armenian leaders such as Yeprem Khan and Khetcho took part, as did the American teacher Howard Baskerville, who was killed. The Bakhtiyari leaders joined the Tabriz rebels, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardar">Sardar</a> Assad's forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> in 1909. Vladimir Liakhov, a Russian colonel, commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-cossack-brigade">Persian Cossack Brigade</a> that shelled parliament.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/national-consultative-assembly"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-consultative-assembly.jpg" alt="National Consultative Assembly" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/national-consultative-assembly">National Consultative Assembly</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_inaugural_National_Consultative_Assembly_of_Iran.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Supersian, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The revolution produced a parliament with final approval of all loans and the budget. The fundamental laws of 30 December 1906 provided for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-consultative-assembly">National Consultative Assembly</a> and a Senate, and the Constitutional Amendment of 1907 declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a> the state religion and called for a council of five high-ranking Twelver Shia clerics to ensure that parliament's laws did not contradict the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The electoral law of 9 September 1906 excluded women, foreigners, men under 25 and others from voting. The constitution, though not adhered to, remained in force until after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Islamic Revolution</a>.</p><p>Violence marked the struggle on both sides. In Zanjan Mulla Qurban Ali Zanjani is reported to have mobilised six hundred men who looted the shops of pro-democracy merchants and killed the representative Sa'd al-Saltanih. After the march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> a Revolutionary Tribunal found Nouri guilty of inciting mobs and of issuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a> that declared parliamentary leaders apostates whose blood ought to be shed, and in July 1909 he was hanged as a traitor. The movement then split: in 1910 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sattar-khan">Sattar Khan</a> refused an order to disarm and was wounded when Yeprem Khan, the police chief of Tehran, disarmed his fighters.</p><p>The revolution ended in December 1911, when the deputies of the Second Majlis, weakened by internal dissension, the apathy of the masses, the antagonism of the upper class and the open enmity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, were roughly expelled and threatened with death if they returned by the shah's cabinet, backed by 12,000 Russian troops.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The legacy of the revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> is mixed. It has been called an epoch-making episode in the modern history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and the first of its kind in the Islamic world, earlier than the Young Turk revolution of 1908. It opened the way for debate in a burgeoning press and for new institutions in place of the old order. After the 1921 coup, parliament amended the constitution on 12 December 1925, replacing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a> with the Pahlavi dynasty as the legitimate sovereigns of Iran. The 1906–1907 constitution remained until a new one was approved in a referendum on 2 and 3 December 1979 that established an Islamic republic.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a>, leader of the 1979 revolution, who was a child at the time, held that the constitution of 1906 was the work of agents of imperialist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, who he said had borrowed a Belgian legal code and added some ordinances of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in order to deceive the people. After the Iranian revolution Nouri was celebrated enough in the Islamic Republic to have an expressway named after him, though he had defended the monarchy against the constitution. Reza Aslan argues that what separates the uprisings of 1906, 1953 and 1979 that resulted in radical change from the countless others of the last hundred-odd years is a coalition of the educated middle class, the mid-level clerics and seminary students, and the bazaari merchants.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/morq-e-sahar">Morq-e Sahar (Bird of the Dawn)<span>c. 1924</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Persian Constitutional Revolution of 1905 to 1911 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> a parliament and a constitution through an alliance of clerics, intellectuals and bazaar merchants against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> shah. It survived a royal coup in 1908 but ended when the Second Majlis was expelled in 1911 with the support of Russian troops.</em></p>

